speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, I have not to find fault with the discussion that has taken place-far from it;
I think it is quite apropos. I am very glad of the observations addressed to the House by the leader of the opposition (Mr. R. L. Borden) and the hon. member for North Toronto (Mr. Foster). I suppose that my hon. friend from North Toronto will not dispute this-that a Department of External Affairs is a necessity of government I suppose that he will not dispute this also -that, some time or other whether now or later, there must be in this government, as in all other governments, a Department of External Affairs. If my hon. friend agrees

with this-and I do not think he can dispute it-I think he has answered all the criticisms which he has offered to the House. The only question which might arise, in my opinion, is, whether or not the time has come when such a department is necessary. If you tell me that Canada has not yet reached such a point in its development that necessity arises for this department, that is a question that we can debate. But if you tell me that under no circumstances should such a department be created, I say that that is a proposition which you cannot maintain, and, I am sure, on reflection, will not maintain. I look upon it as a matter of necessity that when a government which has reached the condition that Canada has reached, such a department cannot be dispensed with.
